From: Gemini PDA Linux bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] soc: mediatek: bring up MT6797's Cortex-A72 cluster

MT6797's two Cortex-A72 cores (cpu8, cpu9) sit behind a power domain fed by an
external DA9214 buck on i2c6, and the firmware refuses to power the cluster
unless that rail, the cluster PLL and an SRAM LDO are all set up before PSCI
CPU_ON. Nothing in mainline does any of it, so onlining cpu8 returns -EIO at
best and hangs the caller inside the secure monitor at worst.

The vendor kernel does this work in cpu_psci_cpu_boot(); do the same from a
CPUHP_BP_PREPARE_DYN callback. With this, cpu8 and cpu9 online through plain
sysfs hotplug and behave as ordinary CPUs.

Once the first core is up, the cluster is retuned from the firmware-gate
750 MHz to 2002 MHz through the firmware's own BIGIDVFSPLLSETFREQ call (the
MCUMIXED CON1 interface is dead at runtime: writes stick in the register and
the PLL ignores them). 2002 MHz measures 2.9x the bring-up value and 1.66x the
fastest A53 single-thread, with ~180 mV margin under the 1180 mV rail; the
silicon's rated 2496 MHz measured stable and is reachable at runtime through
/sys/kernel/mt6797_a72/freq_mhz. Vsram is raised to Android's upper-bin 1.20 V
before any clock past 1 GHz.

Details that are load-bearing and commented in the driver: touch no hardware
at init (a power-controller poke while the machine boots latches a failure for
the whole boot); leave the frequency meter holding a true source-36 reading
(CLK26CALI_1 latches the previous run; the firmware's check is single-shot);
free the CSPM semaphore (the firmware polls it unbounded); first core only
(retuning the PLL under a running core hangs it — hence the backup-mux dance
for runtime retunes); never pre-power the cluster (the firmware's power-up
transition is what withdraws the interconnect's pwrdnack).

The prepare callback also reads the cluster power controller at rest and
refuses to proceed when it is in a bad latched state (a per-boot condition
that no retry clears, and on which CPU_ON would spin forever at EL3). The
refusal surfaces as a failed sysfs write instead of a wedged machine, which is
what makes unattended onlining safe to automate.

---
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-a72.c
@@ -0,0 +1,608 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * MT6797 (Planet Computers Gemini PDA) — bring the Cortex-A72 cluster online.
+ *
+ * This SoC is 4x Cortex-A53 + 4x Cortex-A53 + 2x Cortex-A72. The A53s boot
+ * normally. The A72s (cpu8, cpu9) cannot: their cluster sits behind a separate
+ * power domain fed by an *external* buck on i2c6 (Dialog DA9214 BUCKB), and
+ * the firmware refuses to power it up unless several things are already true
+ * before PSCI CPU_ON is issued. Nothing in mainline knows to do any of that, so
+ * `echo 1 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu8/online` returns -EIO at best and hangs
+ * the caller inside the secure monitor at worst.
+ *
+ * The vendor kernel solves this in cpu_psci_cpu_boot() (arch/arm64/kernel/psci.c
+ * in the 3.18 tree), which runs a cpu_power_on_buck() prologue for cpu8/9 before
+ * calling PSCI. We do the same work, but from a CPUHP_BP_PREPARE_DYN callback:
+ * that runs on the controlling CPU immediately before bringup_cpu(), which is
+ * exactly the vendor's insertion point, and needs no change to generic code.
+ *
+ * The sequence, and why each step is here:
+ *
+ *   1. MP2_CPUSYS_PWR_CON |= PWR_RST_B, and a dummy read of the iDVFS block.
+ *      Both are the vendor's, in that order.
+ *   2. Enable VPROC2 (DA9214 BUCKB) at 1180 mV. This is the A72 cluster's
+ *      supply; without it the cluster cannot be powered at all. Done once at
+ *      probe rather than per-hotplug so the rail has already settled.
+ *   3. Hold the PMIC wrapper's SPI controller in reset across the external-buck
+ *      isolation change (WDT_SWSYSRST bit 11, key 0x88), then release it. The
+ *      vendor brackets the isolation change this way; it is an interlock, not a
+ *      cluster reset — WDT_SWSYSRST has no big-cluster reset bit.
+ *   4. Clear CPU_EXT_BUCK_ISO, then set the big cluster's SRAM LDO to 1.10 V
+ *      through the firmware's own SIP call.
+ *   5. Program ARMCAXPLL2 to 750 MHz. The firmware measures the cluster clock
+ *      with its own frequency meter and spins forever unless it reads
+ *      742500..757500 kHz. This is a real clock change, not a trick: the meter
+ *      reads 749988 kHz afterwards.
+ *
+ * Deliberately NOT done: powering the MP2 CPUTOP ourselves. Doing so makes the
+ * firmware's own PWR_ON a no-op, so the cluster's power controller never runs
+ * its power-up *transition* — and the interconnect handshake that withdraws
+ * MP2's pwrdnack is part of that transition. Pre-powering the cluster is what
+ * made every earlier attempt hang forever on INFRA_TOPAXI_PROTEXTSTA3 bit 10.
+ * Leave the cluster alone and let the firmware raise it.
+ *
+ * Both cores work as ordinary CPUs once up: they run userspace, idle in WFI and
+ * wake from it, and take interrupts normally.
+ */
+
+#define pr_fmt(fmt) "mt6797-a72: " fmt
+
+#include <linux/arm-smccc.h>
+#include <linux/cpu.h>
+#include <linux/cpuhotplug.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/i2c.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/math64.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/sysfs.h>
+#include <linux/kobject.h>
+
+/* System Power Manager: the SoC-internal power switches. */
+#define SPM_PHYS		0x10006000
+#define SPM_SIZE		0x1000
+#define SPM_POWERON_CONFIG_EN	0x000
+#define SPM_KEY			0x0b160001
+#define MP2_CPUSYS_PWR_CON	0x218
+#define CPU_EXT_BUCK_ISO	0x290
+#define PWR_RST_B		BIT(0)
+
+/* MCUMIXED: the big cluster's PLL. */
+#define MCUMIXED_PHYS		0x1001a000
+#define MCUMIXED_SIZE		0x1000
+#define ARMCAXPLL2_CON1		0x224
+#define ARMCAXPLL2_SHADOW	0x268
+
+/* Reset generation unit: only bit 11 of WDT_SWSYSRST is touched. */
+#define RGU_PHYS		0x10007000
+#define RGU_SIZE		0x100
+#define WDT_SWSYSRST		0x018
+#define WDT_SWSYSRST_KEY	0x88000000
+#define WDT_PWRAP_SPI_CTL_RST	0x800
+
+/* DA9214 on i2c6 (adapter 2). Code to volts: mV = 300 + code * 10. */
+#define DA9214_ADDR		0x68
+#define DA9214_PAGE_CON		0x00
+#define DA9214_BUCKB_CONT	0x5e
+#define DA9214_VBUCKB_A		0xd9
+#define VPROC2_MV		1180
+
+/*
+ * CSPM. The firmware takes this hardware semaphore around its own accesses to
+ * the cluster clock dividers, and polls for it WITHOUT A BOUND. If anything
+ * left it held, PSCI CPU_ON never returns and the calling CPU is stuck at EL3
+ * — which is indistinguishable from the cluster failing to power up.
+ */
+#define CSPM_PHYS		0x11015000
+#define CSPM_SIZE		0x1000
+#define CSPM_POWERON_CONFIG	0x000
+#define CSPM_KEY		0x0b160001
+#define CSPM_SEMA		0x448
+
+/*
+ * TOPCKGEN's frequency meter, and the reason we have to use it.
+ *
+ * The firmware measures the cluster clock here before it will power the
+ * cluster, and CLK26CALI_1 on this silicon latches the *previous* run's count:
+ * a single-shot read returns whatever was measured last. The firmware's own
+ * routine is single-shot, so it consumes whatever we leave behind. Leaving a
+ * stale count there sends it into a retry loop that has no bound, and PSCI
+ * CPU_ON never returns.
+ *
+ * So take one real measurement of source 37 (the A72 cluster clock) after
+ * programming the PLL. This is not a trick: the value left latched is the true
+ * current frequency, which is why it has to happen after mt6797_a72_set_clk().
+ */
+#define TOPCKGEN_PHYS		0x10000000
+#define TOPCKGEN_SIZE		0x1000
+#define CLK_DBG_CFG		0x10c
+#define CLK_MISC_CFG_0		0x104
+#define CLK26CALI_0		0x220
+#define CLK26CALI_1		0x224
+/*
+ * Source 36 is ARMCAXPLL2's raw output; source 37 is the cluster clock fed
+ * from it. Measure 36: source 37 reads 0 while the cluster is still unpowered
+ * and its clock gated, which is exactly the state we are in here — the whole
+ * point is that the firmware has not raised the cluster yet. 36 reports the
+ * PLL we just programmed, which is the clock the cluster will run on the
+ * instant the firmware ungates it.
+ */
+#define ABIST_SRC_ARMCAXPLL2	36
+
+/* Firmware SIP calls into the iDVFS block. */
+#define SIP_IDVFS_SRAMLDOSET	0xC20003BFU
+#define SIP_IDVFS_PLLSETFREQ	0xC20003B8U
+
+/* TOPCKGEN's backup-mux exposure bits, used while the big PLL is retuned. */
+#define ARMPLLDIV_MUXSEL	0x270
+#define SIP_MCUSYS_READ		0xC200035FU
+#define SPMC_CPUTOP		0x102222a0
+
+#define A72_CLK_KHZ		750000
+#define MPIDR_CLUSTER_A72	2
+
+/*
+ * Where the cluster runs once it is up.
+ *
+ * The firmware's power-on gate demands ~750 MHz, so CPU_ON always happens
+ * there; afterwards the PLL is retuned through the firmware's own
+ * BIGIDVFSPLLSETFREQ call. 2002 MHz is the default: measured 2.9x the 750 MHz
+ * bring-up value and 1.66x the fastest A53 single-thread, while keeping
+ * ~180 mV of margin under the 1180 mV rail (Android runs ~1.0 V here) and a
+ * sustained power level this heatsink-less, thermal-sensor-less board can
+ * take (blockers.md B-47). The silicon is rated to 2.6 GHz; 2496 measured
+ * stable and bit-exact, and is available at runtime through
+ * /sys/kernel/mt6797_a72/freq_mhz for bursts the user wants to pay for.
+ *
+ * Vsram goes to Android's upper-bin 1.20 V before any raise past 1 GHz.
+ */
+#define A72_RUN_MHZ_DEFAULT	2002
+#define A72_VSRAM_X100MV	120000
+
+/*
+ * Scheduler capacities, from the gbench scorecards (04-docs/bench/): serial
+ * integer throughput per core at each cluster's ceiling, normalised to the
+ * A72 at its default clock. With these set, the capacity-asymmetry pass marks
+ * the domains SD_ASYM_CPUCAPACITY and misfit migration moves long-running
+ * work onto the big cores — which is what turns a faster core into a faster
+ * *machine* for unpinned tasks.
+ */
+/* Scheduler capacities (608/617/1024) are in the DT — see mt6797.dtsi. */
+
+static void __iomem *spm_base, *mcumixed_base, *rgu_base, *cspm_base;
+static void __iomem *topckgen_base;
+static bool a72_ready;
+static bool vproc2_on;
+
+static int da9214_write(struct i2c_adapter *ad, u8 reg, u8 val)
+{
+	union i2c_smbus_data d = { .byte = val };
+
+	return i2c_smbus_xfer(ad, DA9214_ADDR, 0, I2C_SMBUS_WRITE, reg,
+			      I2C_SMBUS_BYTE_DATA, &d);
+}
+
+static int da9214_read(struct i2c_adapter *ad, u8 reg)
+{
+	union i2c_smbus_data d;
+	int r = i2c_smbus_xfer(ad, DA9214_ADDR, 0, I2C_SMBUS_READ, reg,
+			       I2C_SMBUS_BYTE_DATA, &d);
+
+	return r ? r : d.byte;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Bring up VPROC2, the A72 cluster's external supply, and verify it by reading
+ * the chip back. The regulator framework caches what it last asked for and will
+ * report that even when the part disagreed, so the readback matters.
+ */
+static int mt6797_a72_enable_vproc2(void)
+{
+	struct i2c_adapter *ad = i2c_get_adapter(2);
+	u8 code = (VPROC2_MV - 300) / 10;
+	int r, cont;
+
+	if (!ad) {
+		pr_err("i2c6 adapter not available; cannot enable VPROC2\n");
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
+	r = da9214_write(ad, DA9214_PAGE_CON, 0);
+	if (!r)
+		r = da9214_write(ad, DA9214_VBUCKB_A, code | 0x80);
+	if (!r)
+		r = da9214_write(ad, DA9214_BUCKB_CONT, 0x1);
+	if (r) {
+		pr_err("DA9214 access failed (%d); cannot enable VPROC2\n", r);
+		i2c_put_adapter(ad);
+		return r;
+	}
+
+	msleep(20);
+	cont = da9214_read(ad, DA9214_BUCKB_CONT);
+	i2c_put_adapter(ad);
+
+	if (cont < 0 || !(cont & 1)) {
+		pr_err("VPROC2 did not come up (BUCKB_CONT=0x%02x)\n", cont);
+		return -EIO;
+	}
+
+	pr_info("VPROC2 enabled at %d mV\n", VPROC2_MV);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The firmware measures this clock and will not proceed unless it reads
+ * 742500..757500 kHz. CON1 bit 31 is the PCW-change trigger and self-clears
+ * once latched; the shadow register is what the hardware DVFS path reloads
+ * from, so both are written.
+ */
+static void mt6797_a72_set_clk(void)
+{
+	u32 con1 = readl(mcumixed_base + ARMCAXPLL2_CON1);
+	unsigned int posdiv = (con1 >> 24) & 7;
+	u32 pcw = (u32)div_u64((u64)A72_CLK_KHZ << (14 + posdiv), 26000);
+
+	writel(pcw & 0x3fffff, mcumixed_base + ARMCAXPLL2_SHADOW);
+	writel((con1 & ~0x3fffffu) | (pcw & 0x3fffff) | BIT(31),
+	       mcumixed_base + ARMCAXPLL2_CON1);
+	udelay(200);
+}
+
+static unsigned int mt6797_a72_meter_once(void)
+{
+	u32 dbg, misc;
+	int i;
+
+	dbg = readl(topckgen_base + CLK_DBG_CFG);
+	writel((dbg & 0xffc0fffc) | (ABIST_SRC_ARMCAXPLL2 << 16),
+	       topckgen_base + CLK_DBG_CFG);
+	misc = readl(topckgen_base + CLK_MISC_CFG_0);
+	writel((misc & 0x00ffffff) | 0x01000000, topckgen_base + CLK_MISC_CFG_0);
+
+	writel(0x1000, topckgen_base + CLK26CALI_0);
+	writel(0x1010, topckgen_base + CLK26CALI_0);
+	for (i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
+		if (!(readl(topckgen_base + CLK26CALI_0) & 0x10))
+			break;
+		udelay(1);
+	}
+	return (readl(topckgen_base + CLK26CALI_1) & 0xffff) * 26000 / 1024 * 2;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The register half of the vendor's cpu_power_on_buck(), run twice.
+ *
+ * Once at init and once again immediately before CPU_ON, which is what the
+ * bring-up tool does and the only arrangement observed to work: the tool runs
+ * its stage 1 (this sequence), waits, then repeats the whole thing before
+ * issuing the SMC. Applying it only once, immediately before CPU_ON, leaves
+ * the cluster's power controller failing to acknowledge and the firmware
+ * spinning at EL3 — and that failure latches for the rest of the boot, so it
+ * is not something a later retry can recover.
+ */
+static unsigned int mt6797_a72_apply_prereqs(void)
+{
+	struct arm_smccc_res res;
+	unsigned int khz;
+	u32 w;
+
+	writel(SPM_KEY, spm_base + SPM_POWERON_CONFIG_EN);
+	writel(readl(spm_base + MP2_CPUSYS_PWR_CON) | PWR_RST_B,
+	       spm_base + MP2_CPUSYS_PWR_CON);
+
+	/* The vendor's dummy read of the iDVFS block, kept in its place. */
+	arm_smccc_smc(SIP_MCUSYS_READ, 0x102224a0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, &res);
+
+	w = readl(rgu_base + WDT_SWSYSRST);
+	writel(w | WDT_SWSYSRST_KEY | WDT_PWRAP_SPI_CTL_RST,
+	       rgu_base + WDT_SWSYSRST);
+
+	writel(readl(spm_base + CPU_EXT_BUCK_ISO) & ~0x3u,
+	       spm_base + CPU_EXT_BUCK_ISO);
+
+	w = readl(rgu_base + WDT_SWSYSRST);
+	writel((w & ~(u32)WDT_PWRAP_SPI_CTL_RST) | WDT_SWSYSRST_KEY,
+	       rgu_base + WDT_SWSYSRST);
+
+	udelay(240);
+	arm_smccc_smc(SIP_IDVFS_SRAMLDOSET, 110000, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, &res);
+	udelay(240);
+
+	mt6797_a72_set_clk();
+
+	/*
+	 * Two measurements: the first flushes whatever stale count was latched,
+	 * the second leaves a fresh, true reading for the firmware to consume.
+	 */
+	mt6797_a72_meter_once();
+	khz = mt6797_a72_meter_once();
+	if (khz < 742500 || khz > 757500)
+		pr_warn("cluster clock measures %u kHz, outside the firmware's %u..%u window; CPU_ON may not return\n",
+			khz, 742500, 757500);
+
+	/* Hand the semaphore back if anything is still holding it. */
+	writel(CSPM_KEY, cspm_base + CSPM_POWERON_CONFIG);
+	w = readl(cspm_base + CSPM_SEMA);
+	if (w & 1) {
+		writel(1, cspm_base + CSPM_SEMA);
+		pr_info("released a held CSPM semaphore (%08x -> %08x)\n",
+			w, readl(cspm_base + CSPM_SEMA));
+	}
+
+	return khz;
+}
+
+static void a72_raise_fn(struct work_struct *w);
+static DECLARE_WORK(a72_raise_work, a72_raise_fn);
+
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(a72_freq_lock);
+static unsigned int a72_cur_mhz = A72_CLK_KHZ / 1000;
+static bool a72_vsram_high;
+
+/*
+ * Retune the running cluster's PLL the vendor's way. The MCUMIXED CON1/shadow
+ * interface goes dead once the cluster is up (writes stick in the register,
+ * the PLL ignores them — measured, the abist meter disagreed with the
+ * readback), so this uses the firmware's BIGIDVFSPLLSETFREQ call, whose EL3
+ * implementation disables the PLL mid-change. The cluster must therefore sit
+ * on the backup mux while it happens — the same MAINPLL-backup dance the
+ * vendor's adjust_armpll_dds() does, under the CSPM hardware semaphore that
+ * ATF itself takes around ARMPLLDIV.
+ */
+static int mt6797_a72_retune(unsigned int mhz)
+{
+	struct arm_smccc_res res;
+	u32 mux, misc;
+	bool sema = false;
+	int i, ret = 0;
+
+	if (mhz < 750 || mhz > 2600)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	mutex_lock(&a72_freq_lock);
+	cpus_read_lock();
+	if (!cpu_online(8) && !cpu_online(9)) {
+		ret = -ENODEV;	/* the firmware call needs the cluster powered */
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	if (mhz > 1001 && !a72_vsram_high) {
+		arm_smccc_smc(SIP_IDVFS_SRAMLDOSET, A72_VSRAM_X100MV,
+			      0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, &res);
+		udelay(240);
+		a72_vsram_high = true;
+	}
+
+	writel(CSPM_KEY, cspm_base + CSPM_POWERON_CONFIG);
+	for (i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
+		writel(1, cspm_base + CSPM_SEMA);
+		if (readl(cspm_base + CSPM_SEMA) & 1) {
+			sema = true;
+			break;
+		}
+		udelay(10);
+	}
+	if (!sema) {
+		pr_err("CSPM semaphore not granted; leaving the clock alone\n");
+		ret = -EBUSY;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	misc = readl(topckgen_base + CLK_MISC_CFG_0);
+	mux = readl(mcumixed_base + ARMPLLDIV_MUXSEL);
+
+	writel(misc | (3u << 4), topckgen_base + CLK_MISC_CFG_0);
+	writel((mux & ~3u) | 2u, mcumixed_base + ARMPLLDIV_MUXSEL);
+	udelay(2);
+
+	arm_smccc_smc(SIP_IDVFS_PLLSETFREQ, mhz, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, &res);
+
+	writel((readl(mcumixed_base + ARMPLLDIV_MUXSEL) & ~3u) | 1u,
+	       mcumixed_base + ARMPLLDIV_MUXSEL);
+	writel(readl(topckgen_base + CLK_MISC_CFG_0) & ~(3u << 4),
+	       topckgen_base + CLK_MISC_CFG_0);
+
+	writel(1, cspm_base + CSPM_SEMA);
+
+	if (res.a0) {
+		pr_err("BIGIDVFSPLLSETFREQ(%u) failed (%ld)\n", mhz, (long)res.a0);
+		ret = -EIO;
+	} else {
+		a72_cur_mhz = mhz;
+		pr_info("A72 cluster now %u MHz\n", mhz);
+	}
+out:
+	cpus_read_unlock();
+	mutex_unlock(&a72_freq_lock);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+/* Runs ON the A72, at the tail of its own bring-up. */
+static int mt6797_a72_online(unsigned int cpu)
+{
+	if (MPIDR_AFFINITY_LEVEL(cpu_logical_map(cpu), 1) != MPIDR_CLUSTER_A72)
+		return 0;
+	if (a72_cur_mhz < A72_RUN_MHZ_DEFAULT)
+		schedule_work(&a72_raise_work);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void a72_raise_fn(struct work_struct *w)
+{
+	mt6797_a72_retune(A72_RUN_MHZ_DEFAULT);
+}
+
+static ssize_t freq_mhz_show(struct kobject *k, struct kobj_attribute *a, char *b)
+{
+	return sysfs_emit(b, "%u\n", a72_cur_mhz);
+}
+
+static ssize_t freq_mhz_store(struct kobject *k, struct kobj_attribute *a,
+			      const char *b, size_t n)
+{
+	unsigned int mhz;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (kstrtouint(b, 0, &mhz))
+		return -EINVAL;
+	ret = mt6797_a72_retune(mhz);
+	return ret ? ret : n;
+}
+
+static struct kobj_attribute a72_freq_attr = __ATTR_RW(freq_mhz);
+
+/* Runs on the controlling CPU, immediately before the target is brought up. */
+static int mt6797_a72_prepare(unsigned int cpu)
+{
+	struct arm_smccc_res res;
+	unsigned int khz;
+	int other;
+
+	if (!a72_ready)
+		return 0;
+	if (MPIDR_AFFINITY_LEVEL(cpu_logical_map(cpu), 1) != MPIDR_CLUSTER_A72)
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * Only the first core of the cluster needs any of this, and the second
+	 * one must NOT get it: the sequence reprograms ARMCAXPLL2 with its
+	 * change-trigger bit, and that PLL is what the already-running core is
+	 * executing on. Redoing it for cpu9 hangs cpu8. The firmware likewise
+	 * skips its own cluster power-up once the cluster is up, and goes
+	 * straight to the core.
+	 */
+	for_each_online_cpu(other) {
+		if (other != cpu &&
+		    MPIDR_AFFINITY_LEVEL(cpu_logical_map(other), 1) ==
+		    MPIDR_CLUSTER_A72) {
+			pr_info("cpu%u: cluster already up (cpu%u); nothing to do\n",
+				cpu, other);
+			return 0;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (!vproc2_on) {
+		int r = mt6797_a72_enable_vproc2();
+
+		if (r) {
+			pr_err("cpu%u: VPROC2 unavailable (%d); refusing\n", cpu, r);
+			return r;
+		}
+		vproc2_on = true;
+		/* Let the rail settle before the cluster is asked to use it. */
+		msleep(1000);
+	}
+
+	khz = mt6797_a72_apply_prereqs();
+	a72_cur_mhz = A72_CLK_KHZ / 1000;	/* the prereqs park the PLL at 750 */
+
+	/*
+	 * The cluster power controller's state before anything asks it to run —
+	 * and the gate that makes unattended onlining safe.
+	 *
+	 * The controller's at-rest value is a per-boot property: most boots read
+	 * 0x004x0100 (FSM_STATE_OUT, bits [27:23], == FSM_OFF with all-off
+	 * acked), but some come up latched in a bad state (0x04500100 and
+	 * friends), and on those the failure holds for the entire boot — the
+	 * firmware's own retry does not clear it, and a CPU_ON issued anyway
+	 * spins forever at EL3, taking the calling CPU with it. Nothing can be
+	 * read back from a core lost that way; the only winning move is not to
+	 * issue the call. Refusing here aborts the hotplug cleanly (the sysfs
+	 * write returns an error) and costs nothing: everything this callback
+	 * did is idempotent, and the next boot gets a fresh roll.
+	 */
+	arm_smccc_smc(SIP_MCUSYS_READ, SPMC_CPUTOP, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, &res);
+	if ((res.a0 >> 23) & 0x1f) {
+		pr_err("cpu%u: cluster power controller is in a bad latched state (%08lx) — refusing CPU_ON; this lasts until reboot\n",
+		       cpu, (unsigned long)res.a0);
+		return -EIO;
+	}
+
+	pr_info("cpu%u: ready (MP2=%08x ISO=%08x SEMA=%08x clk=%u kHz SPMC=%08lx)\n",
+		cpu, readl(spm_base + MP2_CPUSYS_PWR_CON),
+		readl(spm_base + CPU_EXT_BUCK_ISO),
+		readl(cspm_base + CSPM_SEMA), khz, (unsigned long)res.a0);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int __init mt6797_a72_init(void)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!of_machine_is_compatible("mediatek,mt6797"))
+		return 0;
+
+	spm_base = ioremap(SPM_PHYS, SPM_SIZE);
+	mcumixed_base = ioremap(MCUMIXED_PHYS, MCUMIXED_SIZE);
+	rgu_base = ioremap(RGU_PHYS, RGU_SIZE);
+	cspm_base = ioremap(CSPM_PHYS, CSPM_SIZE);
+	topckgen_base = ioremap(TOPCKGEN_PHYS, TOPCKGEN_SIZE);
+	if (!spm_base || !mcumixed_base || !rgu_base || !cspm_base ||
+	    !topckgen_base) {
+		pr_err("ioremap failed\n");
+		ret = -ENOMEM;
+		goto err;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Deliberately touch no hardware here. Bringing the rail up or poking
+	 * the cluster's power controller while the machine is still booting
+	 * makes it fail to acknowledge later, and that failure latches for the
+	 * rest of the boot — no retry recovers it. Everything happens in the
+	 * callback, when someone actually asks for the CPU.
+	 */
+	ret = cpuhp_setup_state_nocalls(CPUHP_BP_PREPARE_DYN,
+					"soc/mt6797-a72:prepare",
+					mt6797_a72_prepare, NULL);
+	if (ret < 0) {
+		pr_err("cannot register the hotplug callback (%d)\n", ret);
+		goto err;
+	}
+	ret = cpuhp_setup_state_nocalls(CPUHP_AP_ONLINE_DYN,
+					"soc/mt6797-a72:online",
+					mt6797_a72_online, NULL);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		pr_warn("no online callback (%d); the cluster stays at 750 MHz\n", ret);
+
+	/*
+	 * Scheduler capacities are NOT set here. They live in the device tree
+	 * (capacity-dmips-mhz), because asym_cpu_capacity_scan() runs once at
+	 * sched_init_domains() and again only when arch_update_cpu_topology()
+	 * reports a change — which plain hotplug never does. Capacities set
+	 * from an initcall are therefore invisible to the asymmetry machinery
+	 * forever: cpu_capacity reads back correctly, and SD_ASYM_CPUCAPACITY
+	 * still never appears (measured, build #66-#67). The DT values are
+	 * parsed before domain init, so the scan sees a big.LITTLE machine
+	 * from the first build.
+	 */
+	if (sysfs_create_file(kobject_create_and_add("mt6797_a72", kernel_kobj),
+			      &a72_freq_attr.attr))
+		pr_warn("no sysfs knob; the cluster is fixed at its default clock\n");
+
+	a72_ready = true;
+	pr_info("registered; cpu8/cpu9 can be onlined (the rail comes up on demand)\n");
+	return 0;
+
+err:
+	if (spm_base)
+		iounmap(spm_base);
+	if (mcumixed_base)
+		iounmap(mcumixed_base);
+	if (rgu_base)
+		iounmap(rgu_base);
+	if (cspm_base)
+		iounmap(cspm_base);
+	if (topckgen_base)
+		iounmap(topckgen_base);
+	spm_base = mcumixed_base = rgu_base = cspm_base = NULL;
+	topckgen_base = NULL;
+	return ret;
+}
+
+/*
+ * late_initcall: i2c6 and the firmware SIP interface must both be up, and there
+ * is no reason to hold up earlier boot for a cluster nothing is waiting on.
+ */
+late_initcall(mt6797_a72_init);
